`showLineNumbers` turns on line numbers for a code block. You add it to the fence's language line, with a space before it. You can enable line numbering for your code block by using showLineNumbers key within the language meta string (don't forget to add space directly before the key).[1]

Numbering starts at 1 by default. To start at a different number, attach a value to the key:

```jsx showLineNumbers=3
export default function MyComponent(props) {
  return <div>Foo</div>;
}
```

Outside Markdown, the same option is a prop on the `CodeBlock` component, where it sits alongside `language` and `title`. The props accepted are language, title and showLineNumbers, in the same way as you write Markdown code blocks.[2]

`highlight-next-line` is a magic comment in code blocks. It highlights the line directly below it. Put it on the line above the code you want marked, and the comment itself is stripped from the displayed output. These are called "magic comments", because they will be parsed and removed, and their purposes are to add metadata to the next line, or the section that the pair of start- and end-comments enclose.[1] Your notes show it in use like this:

```js
function HighlightSomeText(highlight) {
  if (highlight) {
    // highlight-next-line
    return 'This text is highlighted!';
  }

  return 'Nothing highlighted';
}
```

Only the `return 'This text is highlighted!';` line gets highlighted. Different comment styles have no semantic difference, only their content does.[2] So the same directive also works with `#`, `<!-- -->`, or `{/* */}`, depending on the language.

In your library, `makeAdder` is the example of a function factory. The page defines it like this:

```js
function makeAdder(x) {
  return function (y) {
    return x + y;
  };
}

const add5 = makeAdder(5);
const add10 = makeAdder(10);

console.log(add5(2)); // 7
console.log(add10(2)); // 12
```

The outer function takes `x` and hands back a new function, which takes `y` and gives back the sum of the two. The page calls makeAdder a function factory, because each call produces a function that adds a specific value to its argument.[1]

The returned functions are closures. Each one keeps the `x` it was created with. add5 and add10 share the same function body, but each stores its own lexical environment: in add5's, x is 5, and in add10's, x is 10.[2]

