Go's slices are one-dimensional, so you build a 2D grid as a slice of slices. A slice of byte slices[1] is written as `[][]byte`, and a 3x3 array, really an array of arrays[1] is written as `[3][3]float64`.

One way is to allocate each row separately:

```go
// Allocate the top-level slice.
picture := make([][]uint8, YSize) // One row per unit of y.
// Loop over the rows, allocating the slice for each row.
for i := range picture {
    picture[i] = make([]uint8, XSize)
}
```

The other way is to make one large backing slice and cut it into rows:

```go
// Allocate the top-level slice, the same as before.
picture := make([][]uint8, YSize) // One row per unit of y.
// Allocate one large slice to hold all the pixels.
pixels := make([]uint8, XSize*YSize) // Has type []uint8 even though picture is [][]uint8.
// Loop over the rows, slicing each row from the front of the remaining pixels slice.
for i := range picture {
    picture[i], pixels = pixels[:XSize], pixels[XSize:]
}
```

Your notes don't say which to prefer in general, but the source says the choice depends on your application[2]. Rows that might grow or shrink should be allocated independently, while a fixed-size grid can use a single allocation.

