**JavaScript closures:** The library defines a closure as a function paired with the variables that were in scope when it was created. This environment consists of any variables that were in-scope at the time the closure was created.[1] Your `makeAdder` example shows this in practice: each returned function keeps its own `x`, so `add5` and `add10` give different results. They share the same function body definition, but store different lexical environments.[2]

**Go function literals:** The Go page on goroutines says the same idea applies there. In Go, function literals are closures: the implementation makes sure the variables referred to by the function survive as long as they are active.[3] Its example starts a goroutine with an anonymous function, and the function still reads `message` and `delay` from the enclosing `Announce` function:

```go
func Announce(message string, delay time.Duration) {
    go func() {
        time.Sleep(delay)
        fmt.Println(message)
    }()  // Note the parentheses - must call the function.
}
```

The library's Go material covers only this case, so it doesn't show how Go function literals behave outside goroutines.

